    "historical": {
      "question": "As of FY2024, what did Intel say about its foundry strategy? Use only the filings.",
      "answer": "- In its FY2024 10-K, Intel described a dual-purpose foundry strategy: manufacture Intel’s own products while becoming a major semiconductor manufacturer for external customers. Intel expected its factories to remain primarily devoted to its own products for many years. [S7]\n- The economic rationale was scale: Intel’s own products would support leading-edge operations and help derisk offerings for external customers. Third-party business would add leading-edge volume and improve monetization of mature technologies and factories. [S7]\n- Intel separated reporting for Intel Products and Intel Foundry beginning in Q1 2024 to improve transparency, accountability, costs, and efficiency. It also announced plans to establish Foundry as an independent subsidiary—not a completed separation—to provide clearer independence for customers and suppliers and more funding flexibility. [S9] [S7]\n- Its competitive proposition combined wafer fabrication, advanced packaging, chiplets, and software/services, supported by an IP ecosystem and committed US and European capacity available to global customers. [S10]\n- The technology roadmap targeted both leading-edge and mature-node offerings. At the filing date, Intel expected volume production of products on Intel 18A in 2025; that was an expectation, not an achieved milestone. [S5]\n- Intel sought customer advance payments and participation in capacity build-outs to secure volume and derisk investment. It also intended to keep using external foundries where their capabilities or capacity supported Intel Products. [S8]",
      "terminal": {
        "type": "complete",
        "data": {
          "status": "completed",
          "verified_citations": true
        }
      },
